findings.recommendation:
Annotate the four sensitive getters (or fields) with @com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonIgnore so any accidental Mono<Bot> -> ResponseEntity serialization path strips them. Example:

    @JsonIgnore
    public String getEncryptedTokenCiphertext() { return encryptedTokenCiphertext; }

Apply to encryptedTokenCiphertext, encryptedTokenIv, webhookSecretHash. tokenSuffix is debatable since D17 says it ships to the frontend deliberately — but it should ship via BotResponse, not via accidental Bot serialization, so annotating it too is safer. This does not affect Spring Data MongoDB persistence (which uses its own MappingMongoConverter, not Jackson).

Add null + blank handling that returns null (Jackson will treat that as "no value") or throws a typed validation exception. Example:

    @JsonCreator
    public static BotStatus fromString(String s) {
        if (s == null) return null;
        return BotStatus.valueOf(s.toUpperCase());
    }

A stricter variant throws IllegalArgumentException with a clear message on null/blank, which GlobalErrorHandler maps to 400 the same way it maps the unknown-value case today.
